103
00:02:37,003 --> 00:02:37,823
Same index,

104
00:02:37,983 --> 00:02:38,843
very different feelings.

105
00:02:39,403 --> 00:02:40,783
What matters most is how long

106
00:02:40,583 --> 00:02:41,123
you hold,

107
00:02:41,303 --> 00:02:42,303
not the week you're in.

108
00:02:43,550 --> 00:02:44,870
So why are stocks falling?

109
00:02:45,470 --> 00:02:45,950
Reason one:

110
00:02:46,090 --> 00:02:46,750
interest rates.

111
00:02:47,370 --> 00:02:48,650
The 10-year Treasury yield,

112
00:02:48,830 --> 00:02:49,730
what the U.S.

113
00:02:49,530 --> 00:02:50,770
government pays to borrow for ten

114
00:02:50,570 --> 00:02:51,010
years,

115
00:02:51,030 --> 00:02:52,450
is at 5.07 percent.

116
00:02:53,331 --> 00:02:54,251
A year ago,

117
00:02:54,331 --> 00:02:55,771
it was 4.12 percent.

118
00:02:56,458 --> 00:02:57,818
Think about it from an investor's

119
00:02:57,618 --> 00:02:58,058
side.

120
00:02:58,198 --> 00:02:59,878
A government bond now pays over

121
00:02:59,678 --> 00:03:00,818
5 percent a year,

122
00:03:00,618 --> 00:03:01,918
with almost no risk.

123
00:03:01,958 --> 00:03:03,438
A stock promises nothing.

124
00:03:03,618 --> 00:03:05,038
So when safe bonds pay this

125
00:03:04,838 --> 00:03:05,278
much,

126
00:03:05,118 --> 00:03:06,238
stocks have to compete,

127
00:03:06,038 --> 00:03:07,738
and investors pay less for them.

128
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:09,770
Reason two:

129
00:03:09,870 --> 00:03:10,310
inflation.

130
00:03:11,010 --> 00:03:13,210
Prices rose 4.1 percent in the

131
00:03:13,010 --> 00:03:13,970
year to October,

132
00:03:14,210 --> 00:03:15,990
according to the Consumer Price Index.

133
00:03:16,804 --> 00:03:17,644
To fight it,

134
00:03:17,664 --> 00:03:19,464
the Federal Reserve raised its interest

135
00:03:19,264 --> 00:03:20,744
rate on September 17,

136
00:03:21,224 --> 00:03:23,384
its first hike since 2023,

137
00:03:23,684 --> 00:03:27,424
back to 3.75 to 4.00 percent.

138
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:29,860
Higher rates make borrowing more expensive

139
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:31,020
for companies and families.

140
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:33,180
That can slow spending and profits,

141
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:34,860
and stock prices follow profits.

142
00:03:35,707 --> 00:03:36,947
It's already hitting wallets.

143
00:03:37,607 --> 00:03:39,447
The average 30-year mortgage rate is

144
00:03:39,247 --> 00:03:40,827
7.28 percent,

145
00:03:40,627 --> 00:03:42,747
up from 6.24 a year ago,

146
00:03:42,727 --> 00:03:43,707
according to Freddie Mac.

147
00:03:44,087 --> 00:03:44,887
And the prime rate,

148
00:03:44,907 --> 00:03:46,187
the base for most credit card

149
00:03:45,987 --> 00:03:46,487
rates,

150
00:03:46,527 --> 00:03:47,627
is 7 percent.

151
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:49,590
Reason three:

152
00:03:49,710 --> 00:03:51,170
the stocks that rose the most

153
00:03:50,970 --> 00:03:52,150
are falling the most.

154
00:03:52,770 --> 00:03:54,370
Look at the tech-heavy Nasdaq against

155
00:03:54,170 --> 00:03:55,150
the Dow this year.

156
00:03:55,751 --> 00:03:57,191
The Nasdaq ran up faster,

157
00:03:57,311 --> 00:03:58,931
and since its October record it

158
00:03:58,731 --> 00:04:00,831
has fallen 13.5 percent.

159
00:04:00,631 --> 00:04:02,431
When investors take profits,

160
00:04:02,611 --> 00:04:04,391
the biggest winners usually drop first,

161
00:04:04,511 --> 00:04:05,211
and fastest.

162
00:04:06,090 --> 00:04:07,350
At its peak this year,

163
00:04:07,490 --> 00:04:10,110
the Nasdaq was up 18.7 percent.

164
00:04:09,910 --> 00:04:12,730
Now it's up just 2.7 percent

165
00:04:12,530 --> 00:04:13,410
for 2026.

166
00:04:14,190 --> 00:04:15,430
The Dow was up as much

167
00:04:15,230 --> 00:04:16,770
as 13.1 percent,

168
00:04:16,570 --> 00:04:18,600
and now it's up 2.2.

169
00:04:19,850 --> 00:04:21,050
Now the big question.

170
00:04:21,710 --> 00:04:22,790
Does a drop like this turn

171
00:04:22,590 --> 00:04:23,310
into a crash?

172
00:04:23,850 --> 00:04:25,470
We looked at every S&P 500

173
00:04:25,270 --> 00:04:27,030
decline since 1950.

174
00:04:27,810 --> 00:04:28,590
37 times,

175
00:04:28,590 --> 00:04:30,330
the index fell 8 percent or

176
00:04:30,130 --> 00:04:31,070
more from a record.

177
00:04:31,762 --> 00:04:33,682
26 of those kept going to

178
00:04:33,482 --> 00:04:34,642
10 percent or more.

179
00:04:35,138 --> 00:04:36,518
And 11 became bear markets,

180
00:04:36,618 --> 00:04:37,858
with drops of 20 percent or

181
00:04:37,658 --> 00:04:38,058
more.

182
00:04:38,514 --> 00:04:40,154
So roughly 7 out of 10

183
00:04:39,954 --> 00:04:41,934
drops that reached 8 percent never

184
00:04:41,734 --> 00:04:42,814
became bear markets.

185
00:04:43,474 --> 00:04:44,974
But about 3 in 10 did,

186
00:04:45,134 --> 00:04:47,094
so nobody can promise this one

187
00:04:46,894 --> 00:04:47,674
stops here.

188
00:04:48,983 --> 00:04:50,543
Let's look at the two most

189
00:04:50,343 --> 00:04:51,383
recent big drops.

190
00:04:51,963 --> 00:04:52,563
In 2020,

191
00:04:52,363 --> 00:04:53,743
when the pandemic hit,

192
00:04:53,763 --> 00:04:56,023
the S&P 500 fell 34 percent

193
00:04:55,823 --> 00:04:56,783
in about a month,

194
00:04:56,583 --> 00:04:58,423
to a low on March 23.

195
00:04:59,063 --> 00:05:00,203
It was back at a record

196
00:05:00,003 --> 00:05:01,063
by August 18,

197
00:05:00,863 --> 00:05:02,943
and the year ended 16 percent

198
00:05:02,743 --> 00:05:03,143
higher.

199
00:05:03,803 --> 00:05:05,243
2022 was different.

200
00:05:05,543 --> 00:05:07,323
Inflation and rate hikes pushed the

201
00:05:07,123 --> 00:05:09,343
index down 25 percent by October.

202
00:05:09,723 --> 00:05:10,883
It didn't reach a new record

203
00:05:10,683 --> 00:05:12,383
until January 2024,

204
00:05:12,183 --> 00:05:13,423
more than a year later.

205
00:05:14,014 --> 00:05:15,114
For a typical correction,

206
00:05:14,914 --> 00:05:16,914
between 10 and 20 percent,

207
00:05:16,714 --> 00:05:18,894
the S&P 500 took about 7

208
00:05:18,694 --> 00:05:19,974
months to get from its old

209
00:05:19,774 --> 00:05:21,254
record back to a new one.

210
00:05:21,454 --> 00:05:22,574
Some drops heal fast.

211
00:05:22,754 --> 00:05:23,614
Some take years.

212
00:05:25,117 --> 00:05:26,337
What about losing streaks?

213
00:05:26,697 --> 00:05:27,637
Since 1950,

214
00:05:27,737 --> 00:05:29,777
the S&P 500 has had 116

215
00:05:29,577 --> 00:05:31,637
six-day losing streaks.

216
00:05:32,177 --> 00:05:33,577
The last one before this week

217
00:05:33,377 --> 00:05:34,877
was in April 2024.

218
00:05:35,632 --> 00:05:36,812
A year after those streaks,

219
00:05:36,932 --> 00:05:38,772
stocks were higher 91 times out

220
00:05:38,572 --> 00:05:39,852
of 116.

221
00:05:40,581 --> 00:05:42,161
The median gain over that year

222
00:05:41,961 --> 00:05:43,521
was about 13 percent,

223
00:05:43,321 --> 00:05:44,621
not counting dividends.

224
00:05:45,619 --> 00:05:46,799
But 25 times,

225
00:05:47,019 --> 00:05:48,419
stocks were still lower a year

226
00:05:48,219 --> 00:05:48,719
later.

227
00:05:49,159 --> 00:05:50,499
Streaks tell you how people feel.

228
00:05:50,699 --> 00:05:51,899
They don't predict the future.

229
00:05:52,430 --> 00:05:55,050
That April 2024 streak ended with

230
00:05:54,850 --> 00:05:58,170
the S&P 500 near 4,967.

231
00:05:57,970 --> 00:05:59,630
A year later,

232
00:05:59,430 --> 00:06:01,170
it was about 8 percent higher.

233
00:06:02,483 --> 00:06:04,543
Here's something most people don't know.

234
00:06:05,083 --> 00:06:07,003
Big drops happen almost every year,

235
00:06:07,023 --> 00:06:07,983
even in good years.

236
00:06:08,323 --> 00:06:09,983
The red bars show each year's

237
00:06:09,783 --> 00:06:10,483
worst drop.

238
00:06:10,903 --> 00:06:12,383
The green bars show where the

239
00:06:12,183 --> 00:06:12,843
year ended.

240
00:06:13,441 --> 00:06:14,601
Since 1950,

241
00:06:14,861 --> 00:06:16,281
the typical year had a drop

242
00:06:16,081 --> 00:06:17,861
of about 10 percent along the

243
00:06:17,661 --> 00:06:18,081
way.

244
00:06:18,612 --> 00:06:19,112
And yet,

245
00:06:18,912 --> 00:06:21,512
55 of the last 75 full

246
00:06:21,312 --> 00:06:22,592
years ended higher.

247
00:06:23,983 --> 00:06:24,963
One more pattern.

248
00:06:25,423 --> 00:06:26,743
Of the 10 best days for

249
00:06:26,543 --> 00:06:28,803
the S&P 500 since 1950,

250
00:06:28,883 --> 00:06:30,503
8 came within two weeks of

251
00:06:30,303 --> 00:06:31,843
one of the 20 worst days.

252
00:06:32,540 --> 00:06:33,520
The best day of all,

253
00:06:33,620 --> 00:06:35,280
a 11.6 percent gain,

254
00:06:35,540 --> 00:06:37,200
came in October 2008,

255
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:38,740
right in the middle of the

256
00:06:38,540 --> 00:06:39,480
financial crisis.

257
00:06:40,183 --> 00:06:41,743
That's why selling in a panic

258
00:06:41,543 --> 00:06:42,443
can be costly:

259
00:06:42,703 --> 00:06:44,343
the rebound often comes when things

260
00:06:44,143 --> 00:06:44,723
look worst.

261
00:06:46,150 --> 00:06:47,910
So what decides where stocks go

262
00:06:47,710 --> 00:06:48,330
from here?

263
00:06:48,650 --> 00:06:50,030
Here are four things to watch.

264
00:06:50,650 --> 00:06:50,850
First,

265
00:06:50,850 --> 00:06:52,310
the 10-year Treasury yield.

266
00:06:52,610 --> 00:06:54,060
If it keeps climbing past 5

267
00:06:53,860 --> 00:06:54,450
percent,

268
00:06:54,250 --> 00:06:55,690
stocks face more pressure.

269
00:06:56,030 --> 00:06:56,650
If it falls,

270
00:06:56,830 --> 00:06:57,430
they get relief.

271
00:06:58,109 --> 00:06:58,729
Second,

272
00:06:58,869 --> 00:07:00,229
the next inflation reports.

273
00:07:00,869 --> 00:07:01,129
Third,

274
00:07:01,329 --> 00:07:03,209
the Federal Reserve's meeting in December,

275
00:07:03,009 --> 00:07:04,949
where it decides whether to raise

276
00:07:04,749 --> 00:07:05,589
rates again.

277
00:07:06,189 --> 00:07:06,749
And fourth,

278
00:07:06,989 --> 00:07:08,489
jobs and company profits:

279
00:07:08,729 --> 00:07:10,029
as long as people are working

280
00:07:09,829 --> 00:07:11,109
and companies are earning,

281
00:07:11,449 --> 00:07:12,909
a drop is less likely to

282
00:07:12,709 --> 00:07:13,769
turn into a recession.

283
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:16,650
So what does this mean for

284
00:07:16,450 --> 00:07:16,790
you?

285
00:07:17,250 --> 00:07:19,010
Here's what most financial educators point

286
00:07:18,810 --> 00:07:19,290
to.

287
00:07:19,590 --> 00:07:20,730
Money you'll need in the next

288
00:07:20,530 --> 00:07:22,390
few years usually doesn't belong in

289
00:07:22,190 --> 00:07:22,590
stocks,

290
00:07:22,390 --> 00:07:23,970
because a drop like this can

291
00:07:23,770 --> 00:07:24,970
come at the wrong moment.

292
00:07:25,554 --> 00:07:26,754
For long-term money,

293
00:07:26,734 --> 00:07:28,314
time has mattered more than timing.

294
00:07:28,854 --> 00:07:31,054
Spreading money across many investments limits

